MODETEST(1)                      User Commands                     MODETEST(1)

NAME
       modetest - list and exercise the display manager's mode-setting objects

SYNOPSIS
       modetest [options]

DESCRIPTION
       modetest  talks to the kernel's display manager through /dev/dri/card0,
       prints the mode-setting objects it finds there -- connectors, encoders,
       CRTCs, planes, framebuffers and their properties -- and can drive them:
       set a mode, put a test pattern on the screen, move a  hardware  cursor,
       flip pages in step with the vertical blank.

       With  no options it dumps everything it can read, which needs no privi-
       lege beyond opening the node.  The test options do need the node's mas-
       ter rights, which means nothing else may hold them -- run these from  a
       text  console, not from inside an X session, or the mode set fails with
       EACCES.

       On this system the display manager is the kernel's vmwgfx driver, which
       binds to the VMware SVGA II / SVGA3D device.  Where there  is  no  such
       device there is no /dev/dri at all and this program has nothing to talk
       to; see drminfo(1), which says so in as many words.

OPTIONS
   Query options
       -c     List connectors and their modes.

       -e     List encoders.

       -f     List framebuffers.

       -p     List CRTCs and planes.

   Test options
       -s connector_id[,connector_id][@crtc_id]:mode[@format]
              Set  a mode on the given connectors.  The mode may be written as
              widthxheight[-vrefresh],      as       a       full       timing
              (hdisp,hss,hse,htot,vdisp,vss,vse,vtot-vrefresh),  or  as #index
              to pick the connector's n-th mode.  A test pattern is drawn into
              a buffer of the given pixel format and scanned out.

       -P plane_id@crtc_id:widthxheight[+x+y][*scale][@format]
              Put a test pattern on the given plane.

       -C     Exercise the hardware cursor.

       -v     Flip pages in step with the vertical blank, and report the  rate
              achieved.   This  is the direct measurement of whether vertical-
              blank events arrive; on this system they are  generated  by  the
              kernel at 60 Hz.

       -r     Set each connector's preferred mode.

       -w object_id:property_name:value
              Set one property on one object.

       -a     Use the atomic interface rather than the legacy calls.

       -F pattern[,pattern]
              Choose the fill patterns for the test buffers.

       -o path
              Write a writeback connector's output buffer to a file.

   Generic options
       -d     Drop  master  rights after the mode set, so that another program
              can take them.

       -D device
              Open the given device node instead of searching.

       -M module
              Open the node  belonging  to  the  named  driver,  for  instance
              vmwgfx.

EXAMPLES
       Dump everything the display manager exposes:

              modetest

       Set the first connector's preferred mode and paint a test pattern:

              modetest -M vmwgfx -s 32:1920x1080

       Measure page flips against the vertical blank:

              modetest -M vmwgfx -v -s 32:1920x1080

EXIT STATUS
       Zero when every requested operation succeeded, non-zero otherwise.

SEE ALSO
       drminfo(1), vbltest(1), proptest(1), Xorg(1)

